node_modules/
*.log
.DS_Store

# Database. Excluded for size and churn, not secrecy — it is a binary that
# rewrites on every write, and the -wal/-shm sidecars change constantly, so
# tracking it would make every commit a large meaningless diff.
data/*.sqlite
data/*.sqlite-shm
data/*.sqlite-wal

# --- Deliberately NOT ignored, on the repo owner's instruction ---
#
# .env, data/mintWallets.json and data/walletSettings.json are tracked, so a
# clone is a working install with no reconfiguration. They hold live secrets:
# a burner wallet's private key, the Telegram bot token, and the OpenSea /
# Etherscan / GoPlus API keys.
#
# What that costs, so it is a decision and not an accident: a private repo is
# not a secrets boundary. Anything holding `repo` scope on this account reads
# it, git history is permanent, and if this repo is ever made public or shared
# the key leaks with no way to un-leak it — a wallet key has no reset.
#
# So: keep this repo private, treat the burner as disposable and fund it only
# per-mint, and rotate the bot token and API keys if the repo is ever exposed.

# Runtime state, ignored for the same churn reason as the database — both are
# rewritten by the bot itself and both regenerate. nftHoldings.json is only a
# CANDIDATE list (ownerOf decides membership, so a lost file costs one extra
# OpenSea sweep), and armedMints.json holds intentions that a restart already
# re-reports rather than fires.
data/nftHoldings.json
data/armedMints.json
